WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:03.230
Am I talking too fast?

00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:05.029
No. I don’t think so.

00:00:05.029 --> 00:00:05.750
No? Okay.

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:09.590
The polls reveal some interesting information
about millennials and foreign policy.

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:13.620
First off, millennials are far less likely
than their parents or their grandparents to

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:14.540
agree with the statement:

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:16.830
“America is the greatest country in the
world.”

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:22.269
In fact, only 32% of millennials agree with
that according to a Pew poll from 2014

00:00:22.269 --> 00:00:24.990
whereas 50% of baby boomers agree with that

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:30.130
and 64% of the baby boomers’ parents agree
with that—so millennials’ grandparents—agree

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:30.769
with that.

00:00:30.769 --> 00:00:36.049
When you look at the Chicago Council polling
from 2014, only about 50% of millennials agree

00:00:36.049 --> 00:00:36.949
with the statement that:

00:00:36.949 --> 00:00:38.760
“America should stay engaged in the world.”

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:40.229
What are the implications of this?

00:00:40.229 --> 00:00:45.040
Well, currently we’re seeing much more support
for isolationist and multilateralist policies

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:49.140
and that manifests itself in support for candidates
like Bernie Sanders.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:51.409
What are the long-term implications? That’s
a little bit harder to say.

00:00:51.409 --> 00:00:57.080
Of course, if you look at the baby boomers’
generation in the 1960s and 1970s, they violently

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:58.570
opposed the Vietnam War

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:01.979
but eventually they became more confident
in America’s leadership role.

00:01:01.979 --> 00:01:03.780
Is that something that we’re going to see
from millennials?

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:04.750
It’s too soon to tell.

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:10.070
Do you think that millennials
will eventually come around to valuing America’s

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:12.720
leadership role the way that their parents’
generation did?

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.920
Or do you think that what we’re seeing is
a fundamental shift in the way Americans view

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:17.290
foreign policy?

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:18.830
Let us know in the comments section

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:22.250
and also let us know if there are any other
topics you’d like AEI scholars to cover

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:23.170
in 60 Seconds.

